WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.022 --> 00:00:01.730
SRIKANTH BELWADI: So
something phenomenal

00:00:01.730 --> 00:00:04.790
happened around
this time last year.

00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:09.160
About half of all web
traffic was actually video.

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:11.310
It crossed the 50%
mark when I was looking

00:00:11.310 --> 00:00:13.030
at the stats of YouTube.

00:00:13.030 --> 00:00:18.170
And about 300 million--
sorry, 300 hours of video

00:00:18.170 --> 00:00:20.936
was uploaded every
single minute.

00:00:20.936 --> 00:00:24.890
300 hours every minute.

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:29.250
And Red Bull conducted
the Stratos Jump,

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:33.430
and approximately
eight million users

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:36.215
were watching that stream
simultaneously, a single video.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:42.340
And just last year,
YouTube crossed

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:46.810
about one billion users--
active, unique users--

00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:49.010
every single month.

00:00:49.010 --> 00:00:52.400
This was a big shift
that was happening.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:59.520
And this was not just on the
distribution and the content

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:00.400
delivery side.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:03.070
It was looking at what's
happening on the content

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:04.940
production side, as well.

00:01:04.940 --> 00:01:10.030
So YouTube obviously
delivers low resolution media

00:01:10.030 --> 00:01:11.480
for end user devices.

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:12.750
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:13.916
SRIKANTH BELWADI: Thank you.

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:18.640
But the content that's
produced in the studio

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:20.140
is a very small
part of that, that's

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:22.640
actually watched by users.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:25.270
The high-res 4K
uncompressed video.

00:01:25.270 --> 00:01:28.220
Every studio produces
approximately 20 terabytes

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:31.170
of video every day.

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:35.485
And this content needs to be
probably stored for decades.

00:01:35.485 --> 00:01:36.550
Actually, lifetime.

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:37.850
Probably centuries.

00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:41.220
We cannot imagine
Disney's "Snow White"

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:43.860
some footage being
lost after 100 years.

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:45.310
It's just not possible.

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:47.205
This needs to be stored forever.

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:56.420
So it's not just the
content that's produced,

00:01:56.420 --> 00:02:01.070
but all the post-production
that needs to happen on this.

00:02:01.070 --> 00:02:05.150
Last year, I learned that
"How to Train Your Dragon 2"

00:02:05.150 --> 00:02:08.560
required about 75
million computers

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:11.770
to render all the
frames with about 250 TB

00:02:11.770 --> 00:02:14.946
of active disk space.

00:02:14.946 --> 00:02:18.100
And probably more recent
films require even more

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:18.770
compute power.

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:30.650
All of this could have been
a very tractable problem--

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:33.650
huge amount of data, huge
amount of processing--

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:36.470
if not for this graph.

00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:39.940
If it was a uniform
distribution of computers

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:42.530
over a period of
about one year or so,

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:44.120
all the studios
could have probably

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:48.130
gone ahead and built larger and
larger data centers, whether

00:02:48.130 --> 00:02:49.580
[INAUDIBLE] their
business or not,

00:02:49.580 --> 00:02:53.920
and still been able to
solve for this problem.

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:57.940
Look at how the rendering
hours was actually

00:02:57.940 --> 00:03:00.880
used by one of the studios over
a period of six months of movie

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:02.970
production, and this
was how it looked.

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:10.670
This graph is a
nightmare for anybody

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:13.310
who's planning for
capacity or resources.

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:19.480
But it also makes a
case for public cloud,

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:21.690
where you can get
elastic resources,

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:25.540
where you can spin up hundreds
or thousands of computes

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:27.460
as required and
then spin them down.

00:03:33.590 --> 00:03:37.270
But burstability, or this
bursty nature of traffic,

00:03:37.270 --> 00:03:40.300
is only part of the problem.

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:44.560
This industry is facing
millions of media formats,

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:46.980
with all the codecs and
standards and end user

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:50.980
devices that this content
needs to be delivered to.

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:52.760
Hard release dates.

00:03:58.030 --> 00:04:02.080
Irrespective of how much
resources are required,

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:05.230
the producers have set the
final release date as constant,

00:04:05.230 --> 00:04:07.160
and get the resources
as much as required.

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:13.550
Complex toolchains that
changes all the time.

00:04:13.550 --> 00:04:15.220
In fact, every
production almost seems

00:04:15.220 --> 00:04:17.649
like starting a new company.

00:04:21.050 --> 00:04:23.000
And distributed workforce.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:26.720
Last week I was in India, and
there were so many colleges

00:04:26.720 --> 00:04:31.890
and institutions where visual
effects and animation degrees

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:34.330
and certificates
were being offered.

00:04:34.330 --> 00:04:37.210
There are tax incentives
distributed in different parts

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:37.910
of the world.

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:42.470
So studios are no
longer at one location.

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:46.237
And this workforce, which is
distributed across the world,

00:04:46.237 --> 00:04:48.820
is probably working on the same
project and needs collaborate.

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:54.830
And somehow, we need to handle
this enormous, enormous data.

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:01.660
And this challenge of
data and processing

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:05.360
is not limited to one stage
of the media lifecycle.

00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:08.170
The challenge is across.

00:05:08.170 --> 00:05:11.230
How do you ingest this
huge amount of content

00:05:11.230 --> 00:05:15.210
into storage to
where the compute is,

00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:17.370
with all the bandwidth
and latency challenges

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:17.920
that we have?

00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:24.370
How do you render all these
frames with these huge compute

00:05:24.370 --> 00:05:25.116
requirements?

00:05:25.116 --> 00:05:27.240
And also, how do you get
the software licenses that

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:30.000
are required, which have all
been probably provisioned

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:34.420
for on-premises till now?

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:37.400
How do you transcode
for this myriad device

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:40.340
formats that are there?

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:41.750
How do you archive this content?

00:05:41.750 --> 00:05:43.333
And it's not just
storing this content

00:05:43.333 --> 00:05:46.210
for a long time at low cost.

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:49.490
How will you be able to retrieve
this content as required,

00:05:49.490 --> 00:05:54.470
and when you need
it, in minutes?

00:05:54.470 --> 00:05:58.230
And distributing to
users who are worldwide.

00:05:58.230 --> 00:06:03.910
So all these are the challenges
that this industry faces.

00:06:03.910 --> 00:06:05.830
And Google Cloud
Platform is actually

00:06:05.830 --> 00:06:09.730
investing substantial
resources and infrastructure

00:06:09.730 --> 00:06:13.680
to address these very problems.

00:06:13.680 --> 00:06:15.904
And the essential
cloud elements,

00:06:15.904 --> 00:06:18.070
which probably we will talk
through through the rest

00:06:18.070 --> 00:06:22.710
of the sessions today
is flexible compute.

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:26.630
How can you spin up, if
you need, as required,

00:06:26.630 --> 00:06:30.780
tens, hundreds,
thousands of VMs,

00:06:30.780 --> 00:06:34.340
and spin down when
you don't need them?

00:06:34.340 --> 00:06:35.970
And pay for exactly
what you use?

00:06:39.970 --> 00:06:43.610
Always available storage.

00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:48.560
High performance block storage
that you find on public cloud,

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:52.732
but also, can you get the
same low cost of archival

00:06:52.732 --> 00:06:54.815
but be able to retrieve
it in a matter of minutes?

00:06:58.260 --> 00:07:00.330
Global networking.

00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:03.950
Goggle has around 70 plus
edge locations and points

00:07:03.950 --> 00:07:06.920
of presence across the
world, and connected

00:07:06.920 --> 00:07:13.254
through massive network,
including submarine fibers,

00:07:13.254 --> 00:07:14.545
and we are not telecom company.

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:20.325
And solutions for big
data and analytics.

00:07:23.650 --> 00:07:25.650
And it's not just the
infrastructure investments

00:07:25.650 --> 00:07:27.210
that are made.

00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:30.790
Over the last year
or so, [INAUDIBLE]

00:07:30.790 --> 00:07:35.830
have launched about
100 plus features--

00:07:35.830 --> 00:07:38.706
externally-facing,
very important,

00:07:38.706 --> 00:07:42.250
large, large features.

00:07:42.250 --> 00:07:45.150
Some of them, for example,
the more recent ones,

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:46.820
very relevant for
the media industry,

00:07:46.820 --> 00:07:48.653
and we will talk about
this through the rest

00:07:48.653 --> 00:07:49.319
of the sessions.

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:59.160
The Nearline
storage, 32-core VMs,

00:07:59.160 --> 00:08:00.390
integration with [INAUDIBLE].

00:08:00.390 --> 00:08:03.820
We'll have a full
session on that today.

00:08:03.820 --> 00:08:05.950
HTTP load balancing and more.

00:08:09.570 --> 00:08:13.060
But probably you don't
need to know any of that.

00:08:13.060 --> 00:08:15.030
And if there are
just three words

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:18.030
that you are going to take away
from all of today's sessions,

00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:18.830
it would be these.

00:08:22.420 --> 00:08:25.280
Our team, we are
working very hard

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:29.390
to make sure that the
media industry can

00:08:29.390 --> 00:08:35.200
get a little bit of simplicity
in their already complex life.

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:37.460
How can we simplify
your workflows,

00:08:37.460 --> 00:08:41.380
whether you're a small time
developer, a freelancer who

00:08:41.380 --> 00:08:43.990
needs probably a plug-in
to their desktop host

00:08:43.990 --> 00:08:47.680
applications, or a
large, complex studio

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:49.365
and you have a hybrid model?

00:08:49.365 --> 00:08:50.740
You have an existing
data center,

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:54.460
but you need to burst to cloud.

00:08:54.460 --> 00:08:59.100
How can we do all this in
a safe and secure manner?

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:03.440
Google has hundreds of
engineers, security engineers.

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:04.440
Many of them are PhDs.

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:07.250
That's their life--
working for you

00:09:07.250 --> 00:09:11.230
to keep your data
safe and secure.

00:09:11.230 --> 00:09:14.670
These are the engineers
who are trained to ward off

00:09:14.670 --> 00:09:16.700
most sophisticated
attacks, including

00:09:16.700 --> 00:09:18.950
from nation-states for Google.

00:09:18.950 --> 00:09:23.887
They're the ones who will be
working on this for you guys.

00:09:23.887 --> 00:09:24.970
And lastly, the economics.

00:09:28.050 --> 00:09:33.460
The real promise of cloud, which
was pay for exactly what you

00:09:33.460 --> 00:09:36.470
used, not for the entire hour.

00:09:36.470 --> 00:09:40.400
If you used it for a
minute, pay for the minutes.

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:43.860
You don't have to make
large commitments.

00:09:43.860 --> 00:09:46.290
The pricing should be simple.

00:09:46.290 --> 00:09:48.730
And if you use more,
you should automatically

00:09:48.730 --> 00:09:52.030
get a lower price without you
having to commit anything.

00:09:52.030 --> 00:09:56.575
So throughout the next
four to six hours,

00:09:56.575 --> 00:09:59.420
we'll try to address
each of these elements

00:09:59.420 --> 00:10:01.010
through multiple sessions.

00:10:01.010 --> 00:10:03.340
And I hope you'll
have a good time.

00:10:07.050 --> 00:10:11.150
Google Cloud Platform
currently powers multiple media

00:10:11.150 --> 00:10:17.760
companies, from small studios,
individual freelancers,

00:10:17.760 --> 00:10:23.870
distributors like [INAUDIBLE],
Framestore, and even YouTube,

00:10:23.870 --> 00:10:27.770
by far the largest media
distribution company out there.

00:10:27.770 --> 00:10:31.958
And we believe what we're
doing can help you, too.

00:10:31.958 --> 00:10:35.685
With this, is let me call
upon Todd Prives, who's

00:10:35.685 --> 00:10:39.750
a Product Manager for Rendering
at Google Cloud Platform.

00:10:39.750 --> 00:10:41.680
Todd came from ZYNC.

00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:43.390
And over the last
six months, I've

00:10:43.390 --> 00:10:47.720
learned so much from
Todd that I don't now

00:10:47.720 --> 00:10:49.860
feel that I'm new to
this [INAUDIBLE] at all.

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:51.310
Todd?

00:10:51.310 --> 00:10:53.760
[APPLAUSE]

